":") -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Drawing","preciseIdentifier":"s:12GraphDrawing0B0V","kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing\/Drawing"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa","kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Vertex"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"text":"Discussion","anchor":"discussion","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"A is the weighted adjacency matrix: parallel edges add their weights, self-loops are","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ignored, and a weight of 0 is no edge. The eigenvalues are sorted ascending with their","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"multiplicity, so a graph with c components has c zero eigenvalues and its axes come from","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"eigenvectors of 0 when c > 1 (which ones is fixed by the solver, not specified). Each axis’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"sign makes its first entry, in ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"text":" order, whose magnitude is at least 10⁻⁴ of its","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"largest positive. Two vertices have y = 0, and one vertex is at the origin. The drawing is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"not rescaled (","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"rescaled(scale:center:)"},{"text":" maps it to a common frame). A directed graph is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"drawn through ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"undirectedView"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Dense: Householder tridiagonalization and the implicit QL iteration, O(n³) time and n²"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Double"},{"type":"text","text":"s, for graphs of up to a few thousand vertices."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Koren, “Drawing graphs by eigenvectors: theory and practice”, Computers & Mathematics with","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Applications 49(11–12), 2005.","type":"text"}]}]}],"type":"unorderedList"},{"name":"Precondition","style":"note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every weight is finite and not negative; ","type":"text"},{"code":"maxIterations","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", when given, is at","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"least 1.","type":"text"}]}],"type":"aside"},{"name":"Throws","style":"note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":""},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"NoConvergence"},{"type":"text","text":" after "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"maxIterations"},{"type":"text","text":" QL iterations in all (30 n when nil), with"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"that count and the off-diagonal entry being reduced, relative to the largest entry of its"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"block. The iteration converges cubically, about two iterations per eigenvalue."}]}],"type":"aside"}]}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/spectralLayout(weight:maxIterations:)"},"references":{"doc://GraphDrawing/documentation/GraphDrawing/GraphProtocols/Graph/spectralLayout(weight:maxIterations:)":{"fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"spectralLayout","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"(","kind":"text"},{"text":"weight","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":": (","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Edges","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P5EdgesQa"},{"text":".","k